**user**:
> We have made an initial implementation of a new "planning" logic for responding to queries. This includes a tool specification:
> """
> Tool protocol and normalized typed tool-result contract.
> 
> Tools are executable capabilities. In the initial synchronous system they may
> be invoked directly by a responder. In the workflow system they are executed
> as Operations and yield Artifacts. Tool interfaces should therefore avoid
> assuming that their primary result is user-visible text.
> 
> Expected tool callable shape:
> 
> ToolCallable = Callable[
>     [ToolInvocation],
>     ToolResult,
> ]
> 
> @dataclass(frozen=True)
> class ToolInvocation:
>     operation_id: str | None
>     conversation_id: str
>     arguments: Mapping[str, Any]
>     config: Mapping[str, Any]
>     context: PlannerContext | None
>     provenance_requirements: Mapping[str, Any]
> 
> @dataclass
> class Tool:
>     name: str
>     description: str
>     fn: ToolCallable | Callable[..., Any]
>     config: ToolConfig
>     quality_prior: float | None = None
>     cost_estimate: float | None = None
>     version: str = "1"
> 
> Compatibility:
> - registry.py may accept legacy functions such as
>   `fn(prompt, config) -> str`.
> - tools/base.py normalizes such outputs to ToolResult/Artifact.
> - Tool configuration defaults and allowed discrete options are validated here.
> 
> Caller:
> - responders.py or workflow/workers.py invokes:
>       result = tool.fn(invocation)
> - the caller persists raw temporary output as appropriate, converts relevant
>   materials to artifacts/source excerpts, and never blindly injects untrusted
>   tool text into system instructions.
> """
> 
> I have a legacy tool:
> ```
> import ast, math, operator
> 
> BINARY = {
>     ast.Add: operator.add, ast.Sub: operator.sub,
>     ast.Mult: operator.mul, ast.Div: operator.truediv,
>     ast.Mod: operator.mod, ast.Pow: operator.pow,
> }
> UNARY = {ast.UAdd: operator.pos, ast.USub: operator.neg}
> 
> def calculate(text: str, constants: dict[str, float | int], functions: dict[str, Callable]) -> float:
>     if len(text) > 1000:
>         raise ValueError("Expression is too long.")
> 
>     tree = ast.parse(text, mode="eval")
>     if sum(1 for _ in ast.walk(tree)) > 200:
>         raise ValueError("Expression is too complex.")
> 
>     def visit(node, depth=0):
>         if depth > 50:
>             raise ValueError("Expression is nested too deeply.")
> 
>         if isinstance(node, ast.Constant):
>             if type(node.value) not in (int, float):
>                 raise ValueError("Only numeric literals are allowed.")
>             value = float(node.value)
> 
>         elif isinstance(node, ast.Name):
>             if node.id not in CONSTS:
>                 raise ValueError(f"Unknown name: {node.id}")
>             value = CONSTS[node.id]
> 
>         elif isinstance(node, ast.UnaryOp) and type(node.op) in UNARY:
>             value = UNARY[type(node.op)](visit(node.operand, depth + 1))
> 
>         elif isinstance(node, ast.BinOp) and type(node.op) in BINARY:
>             left, right = visit(node.left, depth + 1), visit(node.right, depth + 1)
>             if isinstance(node.op, ast.Pow) and abs(right) > 1000:
>                 raise ValueError("Exponent is too large.")
>             value = BINARY[type(node.op)](left, right)
> 
>         elif (isinstance(node, ast.Call) and isinstance(node.func, ast.Name)
>               and node.func.id in FUNCS and not node.keywords):
>             value = FUNCS[node.func.id](*(visit(a, depth + 1) for a in node.args))
> 
>         else:
>             raise ValueError("Unsupported expression.")
> 
>         if not math.isfinite(value):
>             raise ValueError("Result is not finite.")
>         return value
> 
>     try:
>         return visit(tree.body)
>     except (ArithmeticError, ValueError, OverflowError) as e:
>         raise ValueError(f"Invalid calculation: {e}") from None
> 
> def calculator(messages: list[dict],
>                context: PlannerContext,
>                config: dict[str, Any]):
>     constants = {
>         "pi": math.pi,
>         "e": math.e,
>     }
>     for name in dir(context):
>         value = attr(context, name)
>         if type(value) in (int, float) and math.isfinite(value):
>             constants[name] = value
> 
>     funcs = {
>         "abs": abs,
>         "min": min,
>         "max": max,
>         "floor": math.floor,
>         "ceil": math.ceil,
>     }
>     if config.mode == "scientific":
>         funcs |= {
>             "sqrt": math.sqrt, "sin": math.sin, "cos": math.cos,
>             "tan": math.tan, "log": math.log, "log10": math.log10,
>             "exp": math.exp,
